                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/physicscontents"} Edit this sidebar
          %p The text
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lord+Kelvin"}> Lord Kelvin
                ,
              %p
                %strong On Vortex Atoms
              %p Proceedings of the Royal Society of Edinburgh, Vol. VI, pp. 94-105.
              %p 1867
              %p
                (
                %a{:href => "http://zapatopi.net/kelvin/papers/on_vortex_atoms.html"}> web
                )
          %p
            presented the hypothesis, developed with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Tait"} Peter Tait
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atoms"} atoms
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+particles"}> elementary particles
            ) are fundamentally knotted
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vortices"} vortices
            in some
            = succeed "-filling" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chemical+elements"} chemical elements
            were classified by
            = succeed "-types." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot"} knot
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#impact"} Impact
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#SimilarityToSkyrmeModel"} Similarity to the Skyrme model for baryons
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#SimilarityWithDescartesThoughts"} Similarity with Descartes’ thoughts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#SimilarityToParticlePhysics"} Similarity to concepts of modern particle physics
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#baryogenesis"} Baryogenesis
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_entries"} Related entries
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#impact Impact
          %p
            As a literal theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            the vortex atom hypothesis lasted no more than 30 years, with Thomson himself giving up on it by 1890 (
            %a{:href => "#Kragh"}> Kragh 02, p. 34
            ). But the vortex atom hypothesis prompted
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Tait"} Peter Tait
            to begin the classification of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knots"} knots
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematical
            field of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+theory"} knot theory
          %p
            This history has at times been invoked as a cautionary tale, to warn against contemporary speculations in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
          %p However, on closer inspection there are some ironic twist and turns to this reading:
          %h3#SimilarityToSkyrmeModel Similarity to the Skyrme model for baryons
          %p
            First, recall that in contemporary understanding (but see below), the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chemical+elements"} chemical elements
            are (not knots in the aether, as Kelvin hypothesized, but ) types of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atomic+nuclei"} atomic nuclei
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bound+states"} bound states
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/protons"} protons
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutrons"} neutrons
            that, in turn, are thought to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bound+states"} bound states
            of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constituent+quarks"}> constituent quarks
            ” (under the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+nuclear+force"}> strong nuclear force
            ”), supposedly described by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+chromodynamics"} quantum chromodynamics
            (QCD).
          %p
            The irony here is that – due to the unresolved “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"}> confinement
            ”- or “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+gap+problem"}> mass gap problem
            ” –
            %em no
            good theoretical derivation of these – or any other
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hadron"} hadronic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bound+states"} bound states
            – from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constituent+quarks"} constituent quarks
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            is available! The
            = succeed "-hypothesis" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constituent+quark"} constituent quark
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computer+experiment"} computer experiment
            (see e.g.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "lattice+gauge+theory#Durr09"} Durr et al. 2009
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/baryons"} baryons
            such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proton"} proton
            remain unexplained (see e.g. the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proton+spin+crisis"} proton spin crisis
            and the
            = succeed ");" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proton+radius+problem"} proton radius problem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LHC"} LHC
            keep detecting a long list of novel hadrons which have neither been predicted nor understood by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            – see e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "confinement#Koppenburg21"} Koppenburg 2021
          %blockquote
            %p “&amp;lbrack;Hadron&amp;rbrack; properties follow from yet unsolved mysteries of the strong interaction” &amp;lbrack;sic&amp;rbrack;
          %p In view of this rather disappointing reality, caution may rather be due with respect to dismissing alternative fundamental approaches out of hand.
          %p
            Indeed, a fairly successful alternative theory for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/baryon"} baryon
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bound+states"} bound states
            exists – the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Skyrme+model"} Skyrme model
            – which arguably is conceptually closer to the intuition behind Kelvin’s “vortex atoms” than to the
            = succeed "-model" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quark"} quark
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimensionality
            and variance of the “knotted structure”:
          %p
            Where a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot"} knot
            (in Kelvin’s proposal) is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isotopy+class"} isotopy class
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/map"} map
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            (hence the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-sphere"}> 1-sphere
            ”) into 3d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/one-point+compactification"} 1-point compactified
            to the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-sphere"} 3-sphere
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Skyrmion"} Skyrmion
            has underlying it the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+class"} homotopy class
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/map"} map
            from 3d space/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-sphere"} 3-sphere
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} group manifold
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SU%282%29"} SU(2)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} is
            itself a 3-sphere. Concretely, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+class"} homotopy class
            of this
            = succeed "-map" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Skyrmion"} Skyrmion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pion"} pion
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/baryon"} baryon
            state as a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/soliton"} soliton
            in a spacetime-filling
            = succeed "-field," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pion"} pion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/periodic+table+of+chemical+elements"} periodic table of chemical elements
            – being the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/winding+number"} winding number
            of the map!
          %p
            (Notice that it is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain"} codomain
            3-sphere which is fixed by the physics in this picture, not the domain: On a general
            = succeed "-manifold" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            = succeed "-number" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Skyrmion"} Skyrmion
            = succeed "-set," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cohomotopy"} Cohomotopy
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vanishing+at+infinity"} vanishing at infinity
          %p
            Therefore, it is not hard to imagine that Kelvin would have embraced this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Skyrmion"} Skyrmion
            picture of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atoms"} atoms
            as confirming the basic intuition behind his proposal. To the extent that the
            = succeed "-model" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Skyrmion"} Skyrmion
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/baryons"} baryons
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            beyond plain
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+theory"} knot theory
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#RanadaTrueba01"} Ranada-Trueba 01, p. 200
          %blockquote
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tony+Skyrme"} Skyrme
              had studied with attention Kelvin’s ideas on vortex atoms.
          %p
            To add to the irony, it looks like the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Skyrme+model"} Skyrme model
            comes to itself (only) in a context of
            = succeed "-like" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Randall-Sundrum+model"} Randall-Sundum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+theory"} Kaluza-Klein theory
            and/or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            as well as conceptual explanation, potentially connecting it, logically, to all the rest of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
          %p
            Namely, the original model proposed by
            %a{:href => "skyrmion#Skyrme62"} Skryme 1962
            provided a decent qualitative match to low-lying
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/baryons"} baryons
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binding+energies"} binding energies
            quickly deviated from observation as the baryon number increased. This was eventually fixed (via an observation due to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "skyrmion#AtiyahManton89"} Atiyah &amp; Manton 1989
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} KK-compactification
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instantons"} instantons
            in a higher-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D5+Yang-Mills+theory"}> D=5 Yang-Mills theory
            ):
          %p
            In this picture, the lowest
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} KK-mode
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instantons"} instantons
            of the higher dimensional “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hidden+local+symmetry"}> hidden
            ”
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            reproduces Skyrme’s original model, while the higher KK-modes provide corrections (corresponding to adjoining
            = succeed "-contributions" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+meson"} vector meson
            = succeed "-field)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pion"} pion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binding+energies"} binding energies
            ever closer to the observed values (
            %a{:href => "skyrmion#NayaSutcliffe18a"}> Naya &amp; Sutcliffe 2918a
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "skyrmion#NayaSutcliffe18b"} 2018b
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "skyrmion#ReferencesHadronsAsKKModesOf5dYangMillsTheory"} hadrons as KK-modes of 5d Yang-Mills theory
          %p
            Moreover, this higher-dimensional refinement of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Skyrme+model"} Skyrme model
            naturally appears within
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersecting+brane+models"} intersecting brane models
            within
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witten-Sakai-Sugimoto+model"} Witten-Sakai-Sugimoto model
            for realizing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+chromodynamics"} quantum chromodynamics
            on branes in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+field+theory"} non-perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confined
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+chromodynamics"} quantum chromodynamics
            (including not just
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/baryons"} baryons
            but also
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mesons"} mesons
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/glueballs"} glueballs
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+QCD"} holographic QCD
          %p
            (In final irony, these results remain almost as underappreciated among
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theorists
            as among the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle+physics"} particle
            = succeed "-community" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/high+energy+physics"} high energy physics
          %p
            Further reading along these lines may be found in
            %a{:href => "skyrmion#RhoZahed16"} Rho &amp; Zahed 2016
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "skyrmion#Manton22"} Manton 2022
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#SimilarityWithDescartesThoughts Similarity with Descartes’ thoughts
          %p
            According to the
            %em Routledge encyclopedia of Philosophy
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "http://www.rep.routledge.com/article/DA026SECT11"} here
          %blockquote
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Descartes"} Descartes
              also rejected atoms and the void, the two central doctrines of the atomists, an ancient school of philosophy whose revival by Gassendi and others constituted a major rival among contemporary mechanists. Because there can be no extension without an extended substance, namely body, there can be no space without body, Descartes argued. His world was a plenum, and all motion must ultimately be circular, since one body must give way to another in order for there to be a place for it to enter ( Principles II: §§2–19, 33). Against atoms, he argued that extension is by its nature indefinitely divisible: no piece of matter in its nature indivisible ( Principles II: §20). Yet he agreed that, since bodies are simply matter, the apparent diversity between them must be explicable in terms of the size, shape and motion of the small parts that make them ( Principles II: §§23, 64) (see
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G.+W.+Leibniz"} G. W. Leibniz
              §4 ).
          %p
            However, according to (
            %a{:href => "#Kragh"}> Kragh 02, p. 33
            ):
          %blockquote
            %p In spite of the similarities, there are marked differences between the Victorian theory and Descartes’s conception of matter. Thus, although Descartes’s plenum was indefinitely divisible, his ethereal vortices nonetheless consisted of tiny particles in whirling motion. It was non-atomistic, yet particulate. Moreover, the French philosopher assumed three different species of matter, corresponding to emission, transmission, and reflection of light (luminous, “subtle”, and material particles). The vortex theory, on the other hand, was strictly a unitary continuum theory.
          %h2#SimilarityToParticlePhysics Similarity to concepts of modern particle physics
          %h3#baryogenesis Baryogenesis
          %p
            It is however striking that the modern concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/baryogenesis"} baryogenesis
            via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chiral+anomaly"} chiral anomaly
            and its sensitivity to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instantons"} instantons
            is not too far away from Kelvin’s intuition.
          %p
            To play this out in the most pronounced scenario, consider, for the sake of it, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/no+boundary+proposal"} Hartle-Hawking no-boundary spacetime
            carrying
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+instantons"} Yang-Mills instantons
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton"} instanton
            is in a precise sense the modern higher dimensional and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theoretic
            version of what Kelvin knew as a fluid
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vortex"} vortex
          %p
            Then the non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conservation+law"} conservation law
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/baryon"} baryon
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conserved+current"} conserved current
            due to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chiral+anomaly"} chiral anomaly
            says precisely the following: the net baryon number in the early universe is a steadily increasing number – this is the modern mechanism of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/baryogenesis"} baryogenesis
            – such that as one approaches the asymptotically late time after the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Big+Bang"}> Big Bang
            ” this number converges onto the integer
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
          %p
            Hence while in the modern picture of baryogenesis via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+anomaly"} gauge anomaly
            an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+particle"} elementary particle
            is not literally identified with an instanton, nevertheless each instanton induces precisely one net
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/baryon"} baryon
          %p
            (If one doesn’t want to consider a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/no+boundary+proposal"} Hartle-Hawking-type
            Euclidean no-boundary spacetime but instead a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globally+hyperbolic+spacetime"} globally hyperbolic spacetime
            then the conclusion still holds, just not relative to vanishing baryon number at the “south pole” of the cosmic 4-sphere, but relative to the net baryon number at any chosen spatial reference slice. )
          %p
            For more on this see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "baryogenesis#Exposition"} baryogenesis – Exposition
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Clifford"}> William Clifford
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/On+the+Space-Theory+of+Matter"} On the Space-Theory of Matter
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Skyrmion"} Skyrmion
          %h2#references References
          %p Review and discussion:
          %ul
            %li#Kragh
              %p
                Helge Kragh,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Vortex Atom: A Victorian Theory of Everything
                %strong 44
                1-2 (2002) 32-114 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://philpapers.org/rec/KRATVA"}> philpapers:KRAVTA
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1034/j.1600-0498.2002.440102.x"} doi:10.1034/j.1600-0498.2002.440102.x
            %li#Corfield
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Corfield"}> David Corfield
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Smoke Rings
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/CorfieldVortexAtoms.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samuel+Lomonaco"}> Samuel Lomonaco
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The modern legacies of Thomson’s atomic vortex theory in classical electrodynamics
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Louis+Kauffman"} Louis Kauffman
                (ed.)
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Interface of Knots and Physics
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.csee.umbc.edu/~lomonaco/kelvin/kelvin23.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1090/psapm/051"} doi:10.1090/psapm/051
          %p
            Discussion in comparison to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot"} knotted
            states in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superconductors"} superconductors
          %ul
            %li
              Filipp N. Rybakov, Julien Garaud, Egor Babaev,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Kelvin knots in superconducting state
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.02509"} arXiv:1807.02509
          %p
            Discussion in relation to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skyrmions"} skyrmions
          %ul
            %li#RanadaTrueba01
              Antonio Ranada, J. Trueba,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Force Lines, Vortex Atoms, Topology, and Physics
              %em Topological Electromagnetism with hidden nonlinearlity
              in: Myron Evans (ed.)
              = succeed "," do
                %em Modern Nonlinear Optics – Part 3
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1002/0471231495.ch2"} doi:10.1002/0471231495.ch2
